=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist in the live lab. Helpdesk ticket #7044 describes
Location A VLAN 10 users (PC4/PC5) being unable to reach Location C (pc11–pc14 at
192.168.200.0/24) due to router0's RIP process dying or its egress failing. Direct
measurement contradicts every element of that claim.

Router0's FRR service is active and running. The `show ip rip` output on router0
shows a fully populated RIP table: 192.168.200.0/24 is learned at metric 3 from
neighbor 172.20.1.2 (router5) via eth4 with a fresh timer (2m32s), and all other
topology prefixes are present with up-to-date timers from both WAN neighbors
(10.16.1.2/router1 and 172.20.1.2/router5). Router0's VLAN 10 sub-interface
(eth0.10, 172.16.10.1/24) is UP and is correctly serving as the default gateway
for PC4 (172.16.10.4) and PC5 (172.16.10.5). The egress interface eth4
(172.20.1.1/24 toward router5) is also UP.

End-to-end connectivity tests confirm the path is fully working: PC4 pings both
PC11 (192.168.200.11) and PC13 at Location C with 0% packet loss, and a
traceroute traces the complete 4-hop path PC4 → router0 → router5 → router4 →
PC11 without any drops or timeouts.

Root cause: The ticket is a false premise — no fault has occurred; router0's RIP
process is healthy, its egress interface is up, and Location A VLAN 10 hosts have
full reachability to Location C.

Corrective fix: No remediation is needed on the network. The helpdesk ticket should
be closed as a false alarm; the user should re-test from their actual device to
confirm connectivity is working as expected.
=== END ===
```